window.__ch("<h1 id=\"part-7-product-metrics-data-thinking\">PART 7: PRODUCT METRICS &amp; DATA THINKING</h1>\n<p>A PM who can't reason about numbers is half a PM. A PM who <em>only</em> reasons about numbers is also half a PM. The skill is fluent metric thinking — using data to sharpen judgment, not replace it. This chapter goes deep: the metric frameworks, the analytical methods, the statistical foundations, and the SQL/Python you actually need to operate independently.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-1-the-pm-s-relationship-with-data\">7.1 The PM's Relationship with Data</h2>\n<p>Most PM-data dysfunction comes from one of three patterns:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Dashboard worship.</strong> PMs treat dashboards as truth and stop asking questions. Dashboards lie or mislead more often than people realize.</li>\n<li><strong>Data anxiety.</strong> PMs avoid the numbers because they don't feel confident, then make decisions on vibes.</li>\n<li><strong>Analyst dependency.</strong> PMs can't do basic analysis themselves, so every question takes 2 weeks waiting for a data scientist.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The right relationship: <strong>PMs do their own basic analysis, ask sharper questions of data scientists, and treat metrics as inputs to judgment — not substitutes for it.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>&quot;All models are wrong, but some are useful.&quot; — George Box</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The same applies to metrics. Every metric is a flawed proxy. Your job is to know each metric's flaws, use it anyway, and not be fooled.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-2-the-metrics-hierarchy\">7.2 The Metrics Hierarchy</h2>\n<p>Metrics come in layers. Understanding the layers prevents the most common error: focusing on the wrong level.</p>\n<h3 id=\"level-1-vanity-metrics\">Level 1: Vanity Metrics</h3>\n<p>Numbers that go up and to the right but don't tell you whether you're building real value. Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Total signups (without activation/retention context)</li>\n<li>Total downloads</li>\n<li>Page views</li>\n<li>Total users (lifetime)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These can be useful for marketing, but they should never drive product decisions alone. They're trivially gameable: spend more on ads, signups go up.</p>\n<h3 id=\"level-2-activity-metrics\">Level 2: Activity Metrics</h3>\n<p>Measure usage. Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Daily Active Users (DAU)</li>\n<li>Monthly Active Users (MAU)</li>\n<li>Sessions per user</li>\n<li>Time in app</li>\n<li>Feature adoption rates</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Better than vanity metrics, but still don't tell you if users are getting value. A user can be &quot;active&quot; without being satisfied. Many media companies optimized for time-on-site and accidentally built outrage machines.</p>\n<h3 id=\"level-3-outcome-metrics\">Level 3: Outcome Metrics</h3>\n<p>Measure whether users actually achieve their goals or whether the business achieves its goals. Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Task-completion rate</li>\n<li>Conversion rate at key funnel steps</li>\n<li>Retention rate at week 1, 4, 12</li>\n<li>Revenue per user</li>\n<li>Net Promoter Score (NPS)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are what you optimize. They reflect actual value.</p>\n<h3 id=\"level-4-mission-metrics\">Level 4: Mission Metrics</h3>\n<p>Reflect long-term impact aligned with the company's mission. Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For Khan Academy: students who reach proficiency in subjects.</li>\n<li>For a health app: clinical outcomes (weight loss, HbA1c improvement).</li>\n<li>For a productivity tool: hours saved per user.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are the hardest to measure but the most important strategically. Skipping them is how companies become hollow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-3-north-star-metric-operationalized\">7.3 North Star Metric — Operationalized</h2>\n<p>We covered the concept in Part 4. Now let's get practical about <em>using</em> one.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-north-star-framework\">The North Star Framework</h3>\n<p>Amplitude's framework, widely adopted, structures the NSM into a tree:</p>\n<pre><code>NORTH STAR METRIC\n        |\n        ├── Input metric 1 (e.g., breadth: # active users)\n        |       └── Driver metrics (signups, retention rates, etc.)\n        ├── Input metric 2 (e.g., depth: action per user)\n        |       └── Driver metrics (engagement features, etc.)\n        ├── Input metric 3 (e.g., frequency: sessions per user)\n        |       └── Driver metrics (notifications, return triggers)\n        └── Input metric 4 (e.g., quality: cancellation/error rate)\n                └── Driver metrics (error rate, complaint rate)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Each level cascades. A team owning input metrics drives the NSM through their specific levers.</p>\n<h3 id=\"worked-example-spotify-nsm-tree\">Worked Example: Spotify NSM Tree</h3>\n<p><strong>NSM:</strong> Time spent listening (per user, per week).</p>\n<p><strong>Inputs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Active users (breadth)</em><ul>\n<li>New signups</li>\n<li>Reactivations</li>\n<li>Churn rate</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><em>Sessions per user (frequency)</em><ul>\n<li>Push notification engagement</li>\n<li>Daily routine triggers (morning playlist, etc.)</li>\n<li>Recommendation quality</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><em>Time per session (depth)</em><ul>\n<li>Playlist length / variety</li>\n<li>Auto-play and queue</li>\n<li>Discovery feature usage</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><em>Quality (guardrail)</em><ul>\n<li>Skip rate</li>\n<li>Audio buffer issues</li>\n<li>Subscription downgrade rate</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A PM owning &quot;Discovery&quot; might focus on time-per-session (depth) by improving recommendations. A PM on &quot;Acquisition&quot; focuses on new signups. The NSM connects everyone.</p>\n<h3 id=\"practice-build-an-nsm-tree\">Practice: Build an NSM Tree</h3>\n<p>For each company below, build a 3-level NSM tree:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Cred (Indian credit-card rewards / payments app)</li>\n<li>Notion</li>\n<li>A hypothetical AI legal-research tool for Indian lawyers</li>\n<li>Zomato</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Compare your trees with publicly available info on each company's metrics (when available). Note where you'd disagree with their stated NSM.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-4-the-aarrr-pirate-funnel\">7.4 The AARRR (Pirate) Funnel</h2>\n<p>Created by Dave McClure (500 Startups). One of the most useful, MECE frameworks for thinking about product growth.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-five-stages\">The Five Stages</h3>\n<p><strong>A — Acquisition.</strong> How users first encounter your product.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Channels: organic search, paid ads, social, referrals, content marketing, sales-led.</li>\n<li>Metrics: visits, signups, click-through rates, customer acquisition cost (CAC), channel mix.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>A — Activation.</strong> Whether new users have their first meaningful experience.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The &quot;AHA moment&quot; — when the user first feels the value.</li>\n<li>Metric: activation rate (% of new users who complete the activation event within X days).</li>\n<li>Time-to-activation: how fast does it happen?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>R — Retention.</strong> Whether users come back.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>D1, D7, D30 retention rates.</li>\n<li>Cohort retention curves.</li>\n<li>Long-term retention: do they still use it 6 months later?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>R — Revenue.</strong> Whether users monetize.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conversion rate (free to paid).</li>\n<li>Average Revenue Per User (ARPU).</li>\n<li>Lifetime Value (LTV).</li>\n<li>Expansion / upgrade revenue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>R — Referral.</strong> Whether users invite others.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Viral coefficient (how many new users does each user bring?).</li>\n<li>Referral conversion rate.</li>\n<li>NPS as proxy for likelihood-to-refer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"why-the-funnel-matters\">Why the Funnel Matters</h3>\n<p>The funnel is MECE: every user is somewhere in this lifecycle, and the stages don't overlap. The PM's job is to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Measure each stage.</li>\n<li>Identify the <em>biggest leak</em> — usually one stage dominates.</li>\n<li>Focus there before attacking other stages.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A common mistake: spending heavily on Acquisition while Activation is broken. You bring users in; they never have an AHA moment; they leave; you've wasted money. <strong>Fix the leakiest stage first.</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"worked-example-funnel-diagnosis\">Worked Example: Funnel Diagnosis</h3>\n<p>You're a PM at a B2B SaaS startup. Your funnel:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Stage</th>\n  <th>Metric</th>\n  <th>Current</th>\n  <th>Industry benchmark</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Acquisition</td>\n  <td>Monthly visitors</td>\n  <td>80,000</td>\n  <td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Acquisition</td>\n  <td>Signup rate</td>\n  <td>2.4%</td>\n  <td>2–4% (OK)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Activation</td>\n  <td>Reach AHA moment in 14 days</td>\n  <td>22%</td>\n  <td>35–40% (gap!)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Retention</td>\n  <td>D30 retention</td>\n  <td>35%</td>\n  <td>30–40% (OK)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Revenue</td>\n  <td>Free → paid (60 days)</td>\n  <td>4%</td>\n  <td>2–5% (OK)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Referral</td>\n  <td>Avg invites per active user</td>\n  <td>0.2</td>\n  <td>0.3–0.5 (gap)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Where to focus? Activation. Bringing 22% to 35% (a 60% improvement) cascades through retention and revenue, lifting ARR by an order of magnitude more than tweaking acquisition.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-activation-definition-game\">The &quot;Activation&quot; Definition Game</h3>\n<p>What's the AHA moment? This is the most strategic question for early-stage products.</p>\n<p>Famous examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Facebook:</strong> 7 friends in 10 days. (Facebook's growth team famously identified this in 2006.)</li>\n<li><strong>Twitter:</strong> Following 30 people.</li>\n<li><strong>Slack:</strong> A team sending 2,000 messages.</li>\n<li><strong>Dropbox:</strong> Putting 1 file in a Dropbox folder.</li>\n<li><strong>Zoom:</strong> First successful meeting completed.</li>\n<li><strong>Notion:</strong> First page with structured content created.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To find your AHA moment:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify retained vs churned users.</li>\n<li>Find behaviors that distinguish them (statistically).</li>\n<li>Look for the earliest such behavior — that's the AHA target.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This is statistical work. You'll want a data scientist or to do it yourself with the SQL/Python skills below.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-5-leading-vs-lagging-indicators\">7.5 Leading vs Lagging Indicators</h2>\n<p>A critical concept that's often blurred.</p>\n<p><strong>Lagging indicator:</strong> A measure of <em>what already happened</em>. Tells you the result. Examples: revenue, churn rate, customer satisfaction.</p>\n<p><strong>Leading indicator:</strong> A measure of <em>what is happening now</em> that predicts a future outcome. Examples: support ticket volume, feature adoption, active days in week 1.</p>\n<p>The PM's daily work is largely about leading indicators. By the time lagging indicators move, it's too late to course-correct. But leading indicators give you weeks or months of warning.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Lagging</th>\n  <th>Leading (predicts the lag)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Annual revenue</td>\n  <td>Pipeline coverage, pipeline velocity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Customer churn</td>\n  <td>Engagement decline, support tickets, NPS drop</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Hiring success</td>\n  <td>Application volume, interview pass rate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Product success</td>\n  <td>Activation rate of new users</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Long-term retention</td>\n  <td>Week-1 engagement intensity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A great PM has 2–3 leading indicators they watch daily, and a small set of lagging indicators they review weekly or monthly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"building-a-leading-indicator-dashboard\">Building a Leading-Indicator Dashboard</h3>\n<p>Practical steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>List your top 3 lagging metrics (e.g., 6-month retention).</li>\n<li>For each, hypothesize what early behaviors predict it. (E.g., users who log in 5+ times in week 1 retain at 70%; less than 5 logins, 25%.)</li>\n<li>Validate the predictive relationship with historical data.</li>\n<li>Track the leading indicator daily; lagging weekly/monthly.</li>\n<li>When the leading indicator dips, intervene before the lag manifests.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This is the operational difference between reactive and proactive product work.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-6-cohort-analysis-the-foundational-method\">7.6 Cohort Analysis — The Foundational Method</h2>\n<p>Cohort analysis groups users by a shared characteristic (typically signup week/month) and tracks them over time. It is the single most important analytical method for PMs to master.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-cohorts-matter\">Why Cohorts Matter</h3>\n<p>Aggregate metrics lie. If your DAU is rising but new users are churning faster, aggregate hides the truth. Cohort analysis exposes it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Cohort: users who signed up in week 1.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Week 1: 100% active</li>\n<li>Week 2: 60%</li>\n<li>Week 4: 35%</li>\n<li>Week 8: 28%</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><p>Cohort: users who signed up in week 8 (after some product change).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Week 1 (which is calendar week 8): 100% active</li>\n<li>Week 2 (calendar week 9): 75% (improved!)</li>\n<li>Week 4 (calendar week 11): 50% (improved!)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cohort analysis tells you whether a recent change improved retention for <em>new</em> users — which aggregate DAU couldn't show, because it mixes new and old cohorts.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-a-retention-curve\">Reading a Retention Curve</h3>\n<p>A retention curve plots % of cohort still active vs time since signup.</p>\n<p><strong>The shape matters:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A curve that flattens (e.g., stays at 30% from week 8 onward) → you have product/market fit; users who got value are sticking.</li>\n<li>A curve that decays toward zero → you're failing to retain anyone long-term.</li>\n<li>A &quot;smile curve&quot; that re-rises → resurrection of dormant users (re-engagement working).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Industry benchmarks (rough; varies enormously by category):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>B2C consumer apps: 5–15% D30 retention is typical; 25%+ is excellent.</li>\n<li>B2B SaaS: 60–80% Q1 retention typical; 90%+ is excellent.</li>\n<li>Marketplaces: 20–35% M1 retention typical for both sides.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-power-of-cohort-segmentation\">The Power of Cohort Segmentation</h3>\n<p>Slice cohorts by attribute:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acquisition channel (organic vs paid vs referral)</li>\n<li>User type (free vs trial vs paid)</li>\n<li>Geography</li>\n<li>Onboarding path completed</li>\n<li>First feature used</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Often, one segment retains brilliantly while another doesn't. The product strategy implication: focus on the retaining segment, fix or stop investing in the leaky one.</p>\n<h3 id=\"practice\">Practice</h3>\n<p>For a hypothetical product (or one you have access to), define:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The cohort grouping (typically signup week).</li>\n<li>The retention event (which user action counts as &quot;active&quot;?).</li>\n<li>The retention windows (D1, D7, D14, D30, D60, D90).</li>\n<li>Two segmentations you'd investigate (e.g., by channel, by first feature used).</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"7-7-experimentation-and-a-b-testing\">7.7 Experimentation and A/B Testing</h2>\n<p>A/B testing is how you move from &quot;I think this will help&quot; to &quot;I know this helped (or didn't).&quot;</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-core-idea\">The Core Idea</h3>\n<p>Randomly split users into two (or more) groups. Show different variants. Measure the outcome. The randomization isolates the effect of the change from other variables.</p>\n<p>If done right, an A/B test gives you a <strong>causal</strong> answer. Not &quot;users who saw this also did X&quot; (correlation) but &quot;showing this <em>caused</em> X.&quot;</p>\n<h3 id=\"anatomy-of-a-good-a-b-test\">Anatomy of a Good A/B Test</h3>\n<p><strong>1. Hypothesis.</strong> Stated explicitly, falsifiable.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;Adding social proof badges (e.g., '4.7 stars from 12K reviews') to the product detail page will increase add-to-cart rate by 5–8% (vs 32% baseline).&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>2. Primary metric.</strong> The single number that defines success.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Guardrail metrics.</strong> What must <em>not</em> get worse. (Page load time, support tickets, etc.)</p>\n<p><strong>4. Sample size calculation.</strong> Done <em>before</em> the test. Tells you how many users you need to detect the effect size you care about.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Test duration.</strong> Should run for a full business cycle (often 1+ weeks) to capture day-of-week effects.</p>\n<p><strong>6. No peeking.</strong> You don't look at results until the test ends or hits the planned sample size. Peeking + early stopping is one of the most common ways teams fool themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>7. Pre-committed analysis plan.</strong> Decide before the test how you'll segment, what counts as significant, what you'll do with the result.</p>\n<h3 id=\"statistical-foundations-at-pm-level\">Statistical Foundations (At PM Level)</h3>\n<p>You don't need to be a statistician. You need to understand these concepts well enough to ask the right questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Statistical significance.</strong> The probability that the observed difference is due to random chance, not a real effect. Conventionally, p-value &lt; 0.05 (&quot;less than 5% chance this is random&quot;) = &quot;significant.&quot;</p>\n<p>But: significance alone isn't enough. With huge samples, you can find &quot;significant&quot; effects that are tiny and meaningless.</p>\n<p><strong>Effect size.</strong> How big is the difference? &quot;Conversion rate went from 4.0% to 4.05%&quot; might be statistically significant in a giant sample but business-trivially small.</p>\n<p><strong>Confidence interval.</strong> A range of plausible values for the true effect. &quot;We estimate the lift is +5% with a 95% CI of +2% to +8%.&quot; If the CI doesn't cross zero, you have a significant positive effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Power.</strong> The probability your test will detect a real effect of a given size. Underpowered tests miss real effects (false negatives).</p>\n<p><strong>Sample size formula (rough rule of thumb):</strong> To detect a 5% relative lift in a 4% conversion rate at 80% power and p&lt;0.05, you need roughly 25,000 users per variant. For a 10% relative lift, ~6,250 per variant. There are free calculators online (<a href=\"https://www.optimizely.com/sample-size-calculator/\">Optimizely's calculator</a>, <a href=\"https://www.evanmiller.org/ab-testing/sample-size.html\">Evan Miller's calculator</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-a-b-testing-mistakes\">Common A/B Testing Mistakes</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><p><strong>Stopping early when you see green.</strong> &quot;It's significant after 2 days, let's ship it!&quot; — except the effect often shrinks or disappears with more data. Always run for the planned duration.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Testing too many things at once.</strong> &quot;Let's test 5 variants of the page&quot; → with multiple comparisons, you'll find a &quot;winner&quot; by chance. Either Bonferroni-correct, or test fewer variants.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Ignoring novelty effects.</strong> Users react to <em>change</em> itself. A test that wins in week 1 may regress to baseline in week 4 once novelty wears off. Run long enough.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Forgetting segments.</strong> Aggregate result is &quot;no effect&quot; — but it might be +20% for new users and -15% for power users (canceling out). Segment before concluding.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Sample ratio mismatch (SRM).</strong> Your A/B split is supposed to be 50/50, but ends up 47/53. This is a red flag — something is wrong with the assignment. Always check and investigate before trusting the result.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>A/A test failure.</strong> Run an A/A test (both groups see the same thing). If you find a &quot;significant&quot; difference, your testing infrastructure is broken or your randomization is biased. Healthy teams run A/A tests periodically as a sanity check.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Not calculating sample size in advance.</strong> Running a test on 800 users won't detect a 3% lift, no matter how long you wait. Calculate first.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Cherry-picking metrics.</strong> Running a test, finding the primary metric flat, and then claiming success based on a secondary metric — this is p-hacking. Pre-commit to your primary metric.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Confounded launches.</strong> Running an A/B test simultaneously with a major launch, marketing campaign, or seasonal event. The signal is muddled.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"when-not-to-a-b-test\">When NOT to A/B Test</h3>\n<p>Despite the gospel of &quot;A/B test everything,&quot; sometimes you shouldn't:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low traffic.</strong> If you can't get statistical power, a test will be inconclusive. Use qualitative methods.</li>\n<li><strong>Strategic / brand decisions.</strong> A logo redesign isn't an A/B-testable question; it's a strategic one. Use research and judgment.</li>\n<li><strong>Reversibility.</strong> For one-way doors (e.g., killing a feature with paying customers), testing is risky.</li>\n<li><strong>Speed-critical.</strong> Sometimes a 4-week test isn't justified for a clearly-better change.</li>\n<li><strong>Ethical concerns.</strong> Showing some users a worse experience to test it has ethical limits, especially in healthcare, finance, or vulnerable populations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A great PM knows when to test and when to ship on judgment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-8-product-instrumentation-basics\">7.8 Product Instrumentation Basics</h2>\n<p>Before you can analyze, you must instrument. PMs must know the basics here, even if engineers do the actual work.</p>\n<h3 id=\"event-taxonomy\">Event Taxonomy</h3>\n<p>Define a consistent, structured way of capturing user actions. The standard pattern: <strong>Object-Action.</strong></p>\n<p>Good event names:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>button_clicked</code> (with properties: button_name, screen_name)</li>\n<li><code>search_performed</code> (with properties: query, results_count, source)</li>\n<li><code>payment_completed</code> (with properties: amount, currency, method)</li>\n<li><code>subscription_upgraded</code> (with properties: from_plan, to_plan, source)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bad event names:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>clicked_button_homepage_blue</code> (too granular; explodes the schema)</li>\n<li><code>homepage</code> (no verb; ambiguous)</li>\n<li><code>did_thing</code> (meaningless)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Properties on events let you slice without redefining events. Example: <code>button_clicked</code> with property <code>button_name</code> lets you slice by button later.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-to-instrument\">What to Instrument</h3>\n<p>For any feature:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The entry point (impression / click).</li>\n<li>Each step of the flow.</li>\n<li>Successful completion (the primary success event).</li>\n<li>Each known failure mode.</li>\n<li>Drop-off points (where users abandon).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A good rule: instrument <em>before</em> shipping, not after. If you can't decide what to instrument, you don't yet understand your success criteria.</p>\n<h3 id=\"tools\">Tools</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Product analytics:</strong> Amplitude, Mixpanel, Heap, PostHog (open-source).</li>\n<li><strong>Web analytics:</strong> Google Analytics (GA4), Adobe Analytics.</li>\n<li><strong>Session replay:</strong> FullStory, Hotjar, LogRocket.</li>\n<li><strong>Funnels &amp; cohorts:</strong> Most product analytics tools support these natively.</li>\n<li><strong>Data warehouse:</strong> Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift — for full SQL-based analysis.</li>\n<li><strong>Notebook environments:</strong> Hex, Mode Analytics, Jupyter, Google Colab.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"instrumentation-anti-patterns\">Instrumentation Anti-Patterns</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Instrumenting after the launch.</strong> You miss the early data. Always instrument before.</li>\n<li><strong>Inconsistent naming.</strong> <code>button_click</code>, <code>clicked_button</code>, <code>btn_clicked</code> all in the same app. Adopt a convention; enforce it.</li>\n<li><strong>Property soup.</strong> Hundreds of events with no documentation. Maintain a tracking plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Untracked errors.</strong> If a payment fails, you'd better know. Log errors as events.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"7-9-sql-for-pms-a-working-crash-course\">7.9 SQL for PMs — A Working Crash Course</h2>\n<p>PMs who can write SQL operate at 5× the speed of those who can't. You don't need to be a DBA. You need fluency at intermediate-level analytical SQL.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-pms-should-learn-sql\">Why PMs Should Learn SQL</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Quick ad-hoc questions (&quot;how many users from Mumbai signed up last month?&quot;) become 5-minute self-service instead of 5-day analyst tickets.</li>\n<li>Better intuition for what analytics can and can't do.</li>\n<li>Self-sufficiency in interview prep (analytical questions in PM interviews often test this).</li>\n<li>Earned credibility with data teams.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-to-learn-roadmap\">What to Learn (Roadmap)</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Core SELECT</strong> — SELECT, FROM, WHERE, ORDER BY, LIMIT.</li>\n<li><strong>Aggregation</strong> — GROUP BY, COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, HAVING.</li>\n<li><strong>Joins</strong> — INNER JOIN, LEFT JOIN, the difference and when each matters.</li>\n<li><strong>Subqueries and CTEs</strong> — Common Table Expressions (<code>WITH ... AS</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Window functions</strong> — ROW_NUMBER, RANK, LAG, LEAD, partition-based aggregations.</li>\n<li><strong>Date functions</strong> — DATE_TRUNC, INTERVAL math.</li>\n<li><strong>Funnel and cohort patterns</strong> — specific patterns for product analytics.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>You can become productive in ~20 hours of focused practice. Modes Analytics' SQL tutorial (<a href=\"https://mode.com/sql-tutorial/\">mode.com/sql-tutorial</a>), the SQLBolt tutorial (<a href=\"https://sqlbolt.com\">sqlbolt.com</a>), and DataLemur (<a href=\"https://datalemur.com\">datalemur.com</a>) — built for analytics interviews — are great starting points.</p>\n<h3 id=\"worked-examples\">Worked Examples</h3>\n<p>Assume two tables:</p>\n<pre><code>users (user_id, signup_date, country, plan)\nevents (event_id, user_id, event_name, event_time, properties)\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Question 1: &quot;How many users signed up last week, by country?&quot;</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT \n  country,\n  COUNT(*) AS signups\nFROM users\nWHERE signup_date &gt;= CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL '7 days'\n  AND signup_date &lt; CURRENT_DATE\nGROUP BY country\nORDER BY signups DESC;\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Question 2: &quot;What is the activation rate (defined as making it to onboarding-complete event within 7 days of signup) for users who signed up last month?&quot;</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">WITH new_users AS (\n  SELECT user_id, signup_date\n  FROM users\n  WHERE signup_date &gt;= DATE_TRUNC('month', CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL '1 month')\n    AND signup_date &lt; DATE_TRUNC('month', CURRENT_DATE)\n),\nactivated AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT u.user_id\n  FROM new_users u\n  JOIN events e ON e.user_id = u.user_id\n  WHERE e.event_name = 'onboarding_complete'\n    AND e.event_time &lt;= u.signup_date + INTERVAL '7 days'\n)\nSELECT\n  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM activated)::float\n  / (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM new_users) AS activation_rate;\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Question 3: &quot;Build a 30-day retention cohort table for users who signed up in the last 12 weeks.&quot;</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">WITH cohort AS (\n  SELECT\n    user_id,\n    DATE_TRUNC('week', signup_date) AS cohort_week\n  FROM users\n  WHERE signup_date &gt;= CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL '12 weeks'\n),\nactivity AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT\n    e.user_id,\n    DATE_TRUNC('day', e.event_time)::date AS active_date\n  FROM events e\n  WHERE e.event_time &gt;= CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL '12 weeks'\n)\nSELECT\n  c.cohort_week,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT c.user_id) AS cohort_size,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN a.active_date = c.cohort_week + INTERVAL '1 day' THEN c.user_id END) AS d1,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN a.active_date BETWEEN c.cohort_week + INTERVAL '6 days' AND c.cohort_week + INTERVAL '7 days' THEN c.user_id END) AS d7,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN a.active_date BETWEEN c.cohort_week + INTERVAL '29 days' AND c.cohort_week + INTERVAL '30 days' THEN c.user_id END) AS d30\nFROM cohort c\nLEFT JOIN activity a ON a.user_id = c.user_id\nGROUP BY c.cohort_week\nORDER BY c.cohort_week;\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is intermediate SQL. If this looks intimidating, you'll find each component in tutorials. Practice on real schemas (StrataScratch, DataLemur have PM-relevant datasets and questions).</p>\n<p><strong>Question 4: &quot;Find the top 10 users by transaction volume in the last 30 days.&quot;</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT\n  u.user_id,\n  u.country,\n  COUNT(e.event_id) AS num_transactions,\n  SUM((e.properties-&gt;&gt;'amount')::numeric) AS total_amount\nFROM users u\nJOIN events e ON e.user_id = u.user_id\nWHERE e.event_name = 'payment_completed'\n  AND e.event_time &gt;= CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL '30 days'\nGROUP BY u.user_id, u.country\nORDER BY total_amount DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n</code></pre>\n<p>(The <code>properties-&gt;&gt;'amount'::numeric</code> is JSON-extraction syntax in PostgreSQL — varies by warehouse.)</p>\n<p><strong>Question 5: &quot;Funnel from <code>signup</code> → <code>email_verified</code> → <code>first_action</code> → <code>payment_completed</code> for last week's signups.&quot;</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">WITH cohort AS (\n  SELECT user_id\n  FROM users\n  WHERE signup_date &gt;= CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL '7 days'\n),\ne AS (\n  SELECT user_id, event_name, MIN(event_time) AS first_event_time\n  FROM events\n  WHERE event_name IN ('email_verified','first_action','payment_completed')\n  GROUP BY user_id, event_name\n)\nSELECT\n  COUNT(DISTINCT c.user_id) AS signed_up,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN ev.event_name = 'email_verified' THEN c.user_id END) AS verified,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN ev.event_name = 'first_action' THEN c.user_id END) AS first_action,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN ev.event_name = 'payment_completed' THEN c.user_id END) AS paid\nFROM cohort c\nLEFT JOIN e ev ON ev.user_id = c.user_id;\n</code></pre>\n<p>These five examples cover ~80% of what you'd write as a PM. Memorize the patterns.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-10-python-and-pandas-for-pms\">7.10 Python and Pandas for PMs</h2>\n<p>If SQL gives you data, Python lets you analyze it more flexibly. Pandas is the dominant library.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-pandas\">Why Pandas</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Easier than SQL for some operations (cohort analysis, statistics, plotting).</li>\n<li>Standard for data scientists, so you can read their work.</li>\n<li>Free, with countless tutorials.</li>\n<li>AI tools generate excellent Pandas code from natural-language requests.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-to-learn\">What to Learn</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Loading data (<code>pd.read_csv</code>, <code>pd.read_sql</code>).</li>\n<li>Filtering, selecting (<code>df[df['col'] &gt; x]</code>, <code>df.loc[]</code>).</li>\n<li>Aggregation (<code>df.groupby('x')['y'].mean()</code>).</li>\n<li>Joins / merges (<code>pd.merge</code>).</li>\n<li>Time-series basics (<code>df['date'] = pd.to_datetime(...)</code>).</li>\n<li>Plotting (<code>df.plot()</code> or matplotlib).</li>\n<li>Cohort analysis with pivot.</li>\n<li>Statistics: mean, median, percentile, t-tests via scipy.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"worked-examples-2\">Worked Examples</h3>\n<p><strong>Example 1: Loading and filtering.</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import pandas as pd\n\n# Load data\nevents = pd.read_csv('events.csv')\nevents['event_time'] = pd.to_datetime(events['event_time'])\n\n# Filter to last 30 days, payment events\nrecent_payments = events[\n    (events['event_time'] &gt;= pd.Timestamp.now() - pd.Timedelta(days=30))\n    &amp; (events['event_name'] == 'payment_completed')\n]\n\n# How many payments per user\nper_user = recent_payments.groupby('user_id').size().sort_values(ascending=False)\nprint(per_user.head(10))\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Example 2: Cohort retention table.</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import pandas as pd\n\nusers = pd.read_csv('users.csv')\nevents = pd.read_csv('events.csv')\n\nusers['signup_date'] = pd.to_datetime(users['signup_date'])\nevents['event_time'] = pd.to_datetime(events['event_time'])\n\nusers['cohort_week'] = users['signup_date'].dt.to_period('W')\nevents['event_week'] = events['event_time'].dt.to_period('W')\n\n# Join\ndf = events.merge(users[['user_id', 'cohort_week']], on='user_id')\n\n# Compute weeks since signup\ndf['weeks_since_signup'] = (df['event_week'] - df['cohort_week']).apply(lambda x: x.n)\n\n# Cohort retention table\nretention = (\n    df.groupby(['cohort_week', 'weeks_since_signup'])['user_id']\n      .nunique()\n      .unstack(fill_value=0)\n)\n\n# Convert to retention rate (% of cohort still active)\nretention_pct = retention.div(retention.iloc[:, 0], axis=0) * 100\nprint(retention_pct.head())\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Example 3: Statistical test (A/B test analysis).</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import pandas as pd\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nab_data = pd.read_csv('ab_test_results.csv')\n# columns: user_id, variant, converted (0/1)\n\ncontrol = ab_data[ab_data['variant'] == 'control']['converted']\ntreatment = ab_data[ab_data['variant'] == 'treatment']['converted']\n\n# Conversion rates\nprint(f&quot;Control: {control.mean():.4f}&quot;)\nprint(f&quot;Treatment: {treatment.mean():.4f}&quot;)\nprint(f&quot;Lift: {(treatment.mean() - control.mean()) / control.mean():.2%}&quot;)\n\n# Two-proportion z-test (good for binary outcomes)\nfrom statsmodels.stats.proportion import proportions_ztest\ncounts = [treatment.sum(), control.sum()]\nnobs = [len(treatment), len(control)]\nz, p = proportions_ztest(counts, nobs)\nprint(f&quot;z-stat: {z:.3f}, p-value: {p:.4f}&quot;)\nprint(f&quot;Significant at p&lt;0.05? {p &lt; 0.05}&quot;)\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Example 4: Funnel analysis.</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import pandas as pd\n\nevents = pd.read_csv('events.csv')\n\n# Define funnel stages\nstages = ['signup', 'email_verified', 'first_action', 'payment_completed']\n\n# Get earliest occurrence of each stage per user\nfunnel = (\n    events[events['event_name'].isin(stages)]\n    .groupby(['user_id', 'event_name'])['event_time'].min()\n    .unstack()\n)\n\n# Count users reaching each stage\nstage_counts = (funnel.notna()).sum()\nstage_counts = stage_counts[stages]  # ensure order\n\n# Compute conversion rates between stages\nprint(&quot;Stage counts:&quot;)\nprint(stage_counts)\nprint(&quot;\\nConversion rates:&quot;)\nfor i in range(1, len(stages)):\n    conv_rate = stage_counts[stages[i]] / stage_counts[stages[i-1]]\n    print(f&quot;{stages[i-1]} → {stages[i]}: {conv_rate:.2%}&quot;)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You don't need to memorize syntax. You need to know what's <em>possible</em> and reach for it when needed. AI tools generate this code reliably from natural-language prompts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-11-how-pms-think-differently-from-data-analysts\">7.11 How PMs Think Differently from Data Analysts</h2>\n<p>Both PMs and data analysts work with numbers. But their roles are different.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th>Data Analyst</th>\n  <th>Product Manager</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Goal</strong></td>\n  <td>Accurate description and explanation of data</td>\n  <td>Decision-making for product outcomes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Question</strong></td>\n  <td>&quot;What is true?&quot;</td>\n  <td>&quot;What should we do?&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Skill emphasis</strong></td>\n  <td>Statistical rigor, data manipulation, modeling</td>\n  <td>Synthesis, prioritization, judgment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Output</strong></td>\n  <td>Reports, dashboards, models</td>\n  <td>Decisions, products, outcomes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Standard for action</strong></td>\n  <td>Statistical confidence</td>\n  <td>Decision quality given uncertainty</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Three implications for PMs:</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-data-informs-decisions-data-doesn-t-make-them\">1. Data informs decisions; data doesn't make them.</h3>\n<p>A data analyst tells you &quot;the test was inconclusive.&quot; A PM has to decide anyway. Sometimes &quot;inconclusive&quot; means &quot;kill the feature&quot;; sometimes it means &quot;ship anyway because the strategic value outweighs the statistical noise.&quot; The PM owns the decision.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-speed-often-matters-more-than-perfection\">2. Speed often matters more than perfection.</h3>\n<p>A data scientist might want 6 weeks to do a rigorous analysis. A PM may need a &quot;good enough&quot; answer in 2 days to make a decision. Knowing when &quot;good enough&quot; is enough is a PM judgment skill.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-pms-interpret-in-business-context\">3. PMs interpret in business context.</h3>\n<p>The number is rarely the answer. &quot;Conversion dropped 8%&quot; is a number. &quot;Conversion dropped 8% because we removed credit-card-fields-prefill, which we'll restore by Friday&quot; is the answer. The PM brings the <em>why</em> and the <em>what next</em>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"key-practices\">Key Practices</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Always ask &quot;why&quot; before &quot;how much.&quot;</strong> A drop of 8% in conversion is meaningless until you know the cause.</li>\n<li><strong>Look at distributions, not just averages.</strong> A 7-second average page load could be 3 seconds for 90% of users and 30 seconds for 10%. Different problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Segment before concluding.</strong> Aggregate metrics hide enormous variation across user types, geographies, channels.</li>\n<li><strong>Check the confounders.</strong> Did anything else change? Marketing campaign? Seasonality? Outage?</li>\n<li><strong>Sanity-check the data.</strong> If your DAU jumped 400% overnight, the most likely cause is an instrumentation bug, not a viral moment.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"7-12-worked-case-diagnosing-a-drop\">7.12 Worked Case: Diagnosing a Drop</h2>\n<p>Your DAU was 850K last month. This month it's 770K. CEO asks: <em>what happened?</em></p>\n<p>A junior PM says: &quot;Let me ask the data team for analysis.&quot;</p>\n<p>A senior PM says (within 30 minutes):</p>\n<pre><code>Step 1: Confirm the data is real.\n- Check the dashboard methodology — definition of DAU hasn't changed?\n- Cross-reference with another metric (e.g., events table count of unique users) — same shape?\n- Yes / No?\n  → If no, fix instrumentation. Done.\n  → If yes, real drop.\n\nStep 2: Decompose by acquisition cohorts (MECE).\n- New user DAU: this month 200K (was 250K) → -20%\n- Returning user DAU: this month 570K (was 600K) → -5%\n\nSo most of the drop is in new users. Where's that coming from?\n\nStep 3: Decompose new users by channel.\n- Organic: 100K → 95K (-5%) — small drop\n- Paid: 80K → 50K (-37%) — big drop!\n- Referral: 70K → 55K (-21%) — moderate drop\n\nMost of the new-user drop is paid. Investigate paid:\n\nStep 4: Decompose paid by sub-channel.\n- Google: 40K → 30K — moderate drop\n- Facebook: 30K → 10K — collapse!\n- Other: 10K → 10K — flat\n\nFacebook paid acquisition collapsed.\n\nStep 5: Why?\n- Talk to marketing. Did campaigns change? CPMs spike?\n- Check ad spend: did we cut budget? (Often the cause.)\n- Check Facebook ad accounts: any policy violations / suspensions?\n\nLikely root cause: Facebook ad budget cut + campaign disabled.\n\nStep 6: What action?\n- If budget cut was deliberate → expected, no action.\n- If unintentional → fix.\n- Either way: also investigate why returning user DAU dipped 5%\n  (smaller issue but still worth understanding).\n\nResult: 30 minutes from &quot;DAU dropped&quot; to &quot;Here's the cause and the path forward.&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is what data thinking in PM looks like. Decompose. Segment. Drill. Form hypothesis. Verify. Decide.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-13-comprehensive-practice-exercises\">7.13 Comprehensive Practice Exercises</h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-7-1-nsm-selection\">Exercise 7.1 — NSM Selection</h3>\n<p>For three companies of your choice (one consumer, one B2B, one marketplace), propose:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Their NSM</li>\n<li>4 input metrics</li>\n<li>2 driver metrics per input</li>\n<li>One guardrail metric\nArgue why your NSM is better than alternatives.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-7-2-funnel-diagnosis\">Exercise 7.2 — Funnel Diagnosis</h3>\n<p>A B2C app has these numbers. Identify the biggest leak, recommend top 3 actions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>100K monthly visitors</li>\n<li>5K signups (5%)</li>\n<li>1K activated (D7, 20%)</li>\n<li>600 D30 retained (60%)</li>\n<li>80 paid (8% of D30 retained)</li>\n<li>20 referred 1+ user</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-7-3-a-b-test-design\">Exercise 7.3 — A/B Test Design</h3>\n<p>Design an A/B test for: &quot;Adding a 'price match guarantee' badge on product pages will increase conversion.&quot; Specify:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypothesis with quantified expected lift</li>\n<li>Primary metric, guardrails</li>\n<li>Sample size estimate (assumptions allowed)</li>\n<li>Test duration</li>\n<li>3 ways the test could give a misleading result</li>\n<li>What you'd do if results are flat</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-7-4-sql-practice\">Exercise 7.4 — SQL Practice</h3>\n<p>Write SQL queries for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;Top 10 features used by users who later upgraded to paid (but not by users who churned).&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Week-over-week growth in active users.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;% of users who completed signup → first transaction within 24 hours, by acquisition channel.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-7-5-cohort-analysis\">Exercise 7.5 — Cohort Analysis</h3>\n<p>For an imagined product (or a public dataset), draw out a cohort retention table. Identify whether retention curves flatten (PMF) or decay (no PMF). Suggest 2 interventions if curves don't flatten.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-7-6-drop-diagnosis\">Exercise 7.6 — Drop Diagnosis</h3>\n<p>Your weekly active users dropped 12% over the last month. Walk through your investigation in writing: hypotheses (at least 5), what data you'd pull, in what order, and what action each finding would prompt.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-7-7-building-a-leading-indicator-dashboard\">Exercise 7.7 — Building a Leading-Indicator Dashboard</h3>\n<p>You manage onboarding for a fintech app. Your lagging metric is &quot;% of users who fund their account within 60 days of signup.&quot; Define 4 leading indicators that would predict it within the first 7 days.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-7-8-statistical-reasoning\">Exercise 7.8 — Statistical Reasoning</h3>\n<p>You've run an A/B test. Variant A: 4.5% conversion (n=10,000). Variant B: 4.7% conversion (n=10,000). p-value = 0.43.</p>\n<p>Should you ship variant B? Why or why not? What would change your answer?</p>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"solutions\">Solutions</h3>\n<p><strong>Solution 7.2 — Funnel diagnosis:</strong></p>\n<p>Conversion rates: 5% → 20% → 60% → 8% → 25% (referral).</p>\n<p>Industry-feel benchmarks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Visit→signup 5% — OK.</li>\n<li>Signup→activation 20% — bad. (35–40% typical for healthy products.)</li>\n<li>Activation→D30 60% — strong.</li>\n<li>Paid conversion 8% — varies; not enough info.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The biggest leak is <strong>activation</strong>. Going from 20% to 35% would 1.75× monthly paid users, beating any acquisition or revenue-tier optimization. Top 3 actions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>User research on what blocks new users between signup and AHA — interview 10 churned-at-signup users.</li>\n<li>Audit onboarding flow; identify drop-off step. Likely too many steps before value.</li>\n<li>Test reduced-friction onboarding (e.g., let users use core feature before email confirmation; load sample data on signup).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Solution 7.8 — A/B statistical reasoning:</strong></p>\n<p>You should <em>not</em> ship variant B based on this test alone. Reasons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>p-value 0.43 → no statistical significance. The 0.2pp difference is well within noise range for 10K users.</li>\n<li>The observed difference could just as easily be -0.2pp in a re-run.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What would change your answer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A larger sample with sustained lift and significant p-value.</li>\n<li>Strong qualitative signal (users prefer B in interviews) might justify shipping despite weak data, but be honest about that.</li>\n<li>A cheaper-to-run extended test would clarify.</li>\n<li>Strategic reasons (B is more brand-aligned) might justify shipping anyway, but call it a strategic choice, not a data win.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The PM's job: don't fool yourself. &quot;p=0.43&quot; means &quot;we don't know.&quot; Don't pretend otherwise.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-14-ai-leverage-in-metrics-data-work\">7.14 AI Leverage in Metrics &amp; Data Work</h2>\n<p><strong>Use case 1: SQL generation.</strong></p>\n<p>Prompt: <em>&quot;I have these tables: [paste schema]. Write a SQL query to compute [metric]. Use Postgres syntax. Include comments explaining each step.&quot;</em></p>\n<p>Use AI as a SQL-writing partner. You'll learn faster by reading and tweaking AI's output than by writing from scratch.</p>\n<p><strong>Use case 2: Pandas analysis.</strong></p>\n<p>Prompt: <em>&quot;I have a CSV with columns [list]. Write Python/pandas code to compute [analysis]. Include the cohort retention table style of analysis with a heatmap.&quot;</em></p>\n<p><strong>Use case 3: Statistical interpretation.</strong></p>\n<p>Prompt: <em>&quot;My A/B test: control n=15,000, conversion 8.4%. Treatment n=15,000, conversion 9.1%. Interpret: is this significant at p&lt;0.05? What's the confidence interval on the lift? What sample size would I have needed to confidently detect a 5% relative lift? Should I ship?&quot;</em></p>\n<p><strong>Use case 4: Metric framework brainstorming.</strong></p>\n<p>Prompt: <em>&quot;Help me design an NSM and metrics tree for [product]. Generate 3 distinct alternative NSM choices, with their pros/cons. For my preferred choice, build a 3-level metrics tree with input and driver metrics.&quot;</em></p>\n<p><strong>Use case 5: Drop investigation.</strong></p>\n<p>Prompt: <em>&quot;Our DAU dropped 12% over the last month. Generate 8 hypotheses for the cause, ordered by likelihood given a [type of company]. For each, what data would I pull to test it? What would 'yes' and 'no' look like?&quot;</em></p>\n<p><strong>Use case 6: Dashboard critique.</strong></p>\n<p>Prompt: <em>&quot;Here's my dashboard layout [paste or describe]. Critique: which metrics are vanity vs outcome? Which are leading vs lagging? Which segments am I missing? What would I add for a senior PM to make better decisions?&quot;</em></p>\n<p><strong>Use case 7: Generating event taxonomy.</strong></p>\n<p>Prompt: <em>&quot;I'm shipping [feature]. Help me design an event taxonomy: list each event we should fire, with its properties, and why each event matters for analytics. Use object-action naming. Include events for entry, success, drop-off, and error states.&quot;</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"7-15-references-for-part-7\">7.15 References for Part 7</h2>\n<p><strong>Books:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Lean Analytics</em> — Croll &amp; Yoskovitz. The most practical book on metrics for product. (<a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lean-Analytics-Better-Startup-OReilly/dp/1449335675\">Amazon</a>)</li>\n<li><em>Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments</em> — Kohavi, Tang, Xu. The serious A/B testing reference. (<a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Trustworthy-Online-Controlled-Experiments-Practical/dp/1108724264\">Amazon</a>)</li>\n<li><em>How to Measure Anything</em> — Douglas Hubbard. Estimation and measurement methodology. (<a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/How-Measure-Anything-Intangibles-Business/dp/1118539273\">Amazon</a>)</li>\n<li><em>Practical Statistics for Data Scientists</em> — Bruce, Bruce, Gedeck. PM-friendly stats book. (<a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Practical-Statistics-Data-Scientists-Essential/dp/149207294X\">Amazon</a>)</li>\n<li><em>Naked Statistics</em> — Charles Wheelan. Accessible introduction. (<a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Naked-Statistics-Stripping-Dread-Data/dp/039334777X\">Amazon</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Online courses:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SQL:</strong> Mode Analytics SQL Tutorial (<a href=\"https://mode.com/sql-tutorial/\">free, mode.com/sql-tutorial</a>); SQLBolt (<a href=\"https://sqlbolt.com\">sqlbolt.com</a>); DataLemur (<a href=\"https://datalemur.com\">datalemur.com</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Python/Pandas:</strong> Kaggle Pandas course (free); DataCamp Pandas track; the official Pandas docs.</li>\n<li><strong>Statistics for PMs:</strong> Khan Academy Statistics + Probability (free).</li>\n<li><strong>A/B testing:</strong> Udacity &quot;A/B Testing&quot; course by Google (free).</li>\n<li><strong>Product analytics:</strong> Reforge's Product Analytics course (paid, top-tier).</li>\n<li><strong>Amplitude Academy</strong> — free, structured product analytics curriculum. (<a href=\"https://academy.amplitude.com\">academy.amplitude.com</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tools to learn:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Analytics:</strong> Amplitude, Mixpanel, PostHog (open-source).</li>\n<li><strong>SQL/notebook:</strong> Mode, Hex, Metabase, Looker, Google Colab.</li>\n<li><strong>Spreadsheets:</strong> Google Sheets / Excel (still essential for ad-hoc work).</li>\n<li><strong>Visualization:</strong> Tableau, Looker Studio, Datawrapper.</li>\n<li><strong>A/B testing platforms:</strong> Optimizely, GrowthBook, Statsig, LaunchDarkly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Essays / Resources:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;How to Find Your Aha Moment&quot; — Amplitude. (<a href=\"https://amplitude.com\">amplitude.com</a>)</li>\n<li>&quot;Choosing a North Star Metric&quot; — Sean Ellis, GrowthHackers. (<a href=\"https://growthhackers.com\">growthhackers.com</a>)</li>\n<li>Sequoia Capital's posts on metrics frameworks. (<a href=\"https://www.sequoiacap.com\">sequoiacap.com</a>)</li>\n<li>&quot;Statistics Done Wrong&quot; — Alex Reinhart. (<a href=\"https://www.statisticsdonewrong.com\">statisticsdonewrong.com</a>) — free book on common stats mistakes.</li>\n<li>Evan Miller's A/B test tools and essays. (<a href=\"https://www.evanmiller.org\">evanmiller.org</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>DataLemur — PM-focused SQL practice. (Some free, full access paid.)</li>\n<li>StrataScratch — SQL + Python interview prep.</li>\n<li>Kaggle — data analysis practice on real datasets.</li>\n<li>Set up a personal Mixpanel/Amplitude account on a free tier and instrument a side project.</li>\n<li>Take any product you use, ask &quot;what's their NSM?&quot; and reverse-engineer their metrics tree.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<p><em>End of Part 7. Up next: structured problem-solving and guesstimation — the interview craft, but more importantly, the daily craft.</em></p>\n<p>\\newpage</p>\n");
